    <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-caleo-cajal-dendritic-wiring> a ns1:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Cuntz et al. (2010) PLoS Comput. Biol. & Cuntz et al. (2007) PNAS Ramón y Cajal minimum-wiring and conduction-delay greedy Steiner dendritic trees + Rall's 3/2 diameter power law + (Pass 3) Rall passive-cable electrotonics coupled to a Morris-Lecar dendritic Ca2+ spike hotspot faithfully reproduces Drosophila melanogaster (Class I-IV da sensory neurons), Homo sapiens, Mus musculus & Rattus norvegicus (cerebellar Purkinje cells and cortical pyramidal cells) morphology" ;
        dct:description "The simulation script(s) 2007-caleo-cajal-dendritic-wiring.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Drosophila melanogaster (Class I-IV da sensory neurons), Homo sapiens, Mus musculus & Rattus norvegicus (cerebellar Purkinje cells and cortical pyramidal cells) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 92.2/100, audit 2026-10-19-cajal-dendritic-wiring-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
        dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/45670cb8/2007-caleo-cajal-dendritic-wiring.py> ;
        ns1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/45670cb8/2007-caleo-cajal-dendritic-wiring.py; output: docs/media/cajal_dendritic_wiring_comparison.png; organism: Drosophila melanogaster (Class I-IV da sensory neurons), Homo sapiens, Mus musculus & Rattus norvegicus (cerebellar Purkinje cells and cortical pyramidal cells); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/45670cb8; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: a6977d117c117453a3da70243bb87c40c9868192a748b42afa7ab4730c025791; artifact_sha256: d8a5eea145746b4df580c95c55b83255c17ede5c82986441d0dadf63208aca34; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/cajal_dendritic_wiring/cajal_dendritic_wiring_reference.json=e437ed8419f90b10..., datasets/cajal_dendritic_wiring/cajal_purkinje_dendritic_arbor_specimen.jpg=6255a396655b56c4..., datasets/cajal_dendritic_wiring/electrotonic_ca_spike_reference.json=adf04c13baa46a5e..., datasets/cajal_dendritic_wiring/paper_citations.bib=c8a87e40791184dc..., datasets/cajal_dendritic_wiring/scout_manifest.json=2440e61b14fedf53...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-cajal-dendritic-wiring-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Cuntz, Forstner, Borst & Häusser (2010) PLoS Comput. Biol. 6(8):e1000877 & Cuntz et al. (2007) PNAS 104:13319-13324 greedy Minimum Spanning Tree dendritic growth rule minimizing C(u, v) = ||u - v|| + bf * path(u, root) across balancing factor bf in [0, 1] to balance Ramón y Cajal's (1899) conservation of cytoplasmic wiring length W and conduction time P | Wen & Chklovskii (2008) J. Neurophysiol. 99:2320-2328 & Cuntz et al. (2010) dimensional wiring scaling laws W propto N^(1/2) A^(1/2) in 2D planar arbors and W propto N^(2/3) V^(1/3) in 3D volumetric arbors | Rall (1959) Exp. Neurol. 1:491-527 electrotonic input-conductance impedance-matching power law d_parent^(3/2) = sum_k d_daughter_k^(3/2) and the passive cable length constant lambda_DC = sqrt(R_m d / 4 R_i); Koch (1999) Eq. 2.37 sinusoidal steady-state reduction lambda_f = lambda_DC sqrt(2 / (1 + sqrt(1 + (omega tau_m)^2))) | Roth & Häusser (2001) J. Physiol. 535:445-472 Purkinje passive constants (R_m ~ 122 kOhm cm2, R_i ~ 115 Ohm cm, C_m ~ 0.77 uF/cm2); Llinás & Sugimori (1980) J. Physiol. 305:197-213 all-or-none Purkinje dendritic Ca2+ spikes; Polsky, Mel & Schiller (2004) Nat. Neurosci. 7:621-627 within-branch supralinear vs between-branch linear synaptic summation; Morris & Lecar (1981) Biophys. J. 35:193-213 two-variable Ca2+/K+ membrane; reconstructed_in_script: Full Cuntz et al. (2010) greedy MST solver (`grow_cuntz_dendritic_tree`) across 8 balancing factors bf in [0.0, 1.0] on N = 160 synaptic carriers, reproducing normalized wiring cost W(bf)/W(0) (Pearson r = 1.0000, RMSE = 0.0042) and normalized conduction path P(bf)/P(1) (Pearson r = 0.99969, RMSE = 0.0067), plus a random spanning tree negative control (10.43x wiring inflation) | Log-log OLS scaling law verification across N in [25, 320] at bf = 0.15: alpha_2D = 0.4797 +- 0.0168 (vs 0.5000), alpha_3D = 0.6495 +- 0.0456 (vs 0.6667), bf = 1.0 star limit alpha_star = 0.9996 | Bottom-up Rall 3/2 electrotonic diameter assignment verifying (sum d_daughter^(3/2))/d_parent^(3/2) = 1.0000 (RMSE = 1.27e-16 across 40 bifurcations) | Computer-vision segmentation of Santiago Ramón y Cajal's (1899) human cerebellar Purkinje neuron drawing (`cajal_purkinje_dendritic_arbor_specimen.jpg`, Museo Cajal 328 Madrid): box-counting fractal dimension D_f = 1.7817 +- 0.0248, peak Sholl intersection count = 73 at r/R_max = 0.6176, 62 primary/secondary bifurcation hubs | (Pass 3) Rall/Koch passive-cable electrotonics on every Rall-3/2 edge of every Cuntz tree (X_e = l_e / lambda_f(d_e), lambda_f / lambda_DC = 0.2487 at f_syn = 53.05 Hz), exp(-X) attenuation of two alpha-synapse inputs from the 24 nearest-neighbour terminal-tip pairs to their lowest-common-ancestor hotspot, a vectorised forward-Euler Morris-Lecar Ca2+/K+ hotspot with g_Ca = 0 and frozen-K+ (strictly linear RC) ablations, an inter-input delay sweep dt in [0, 30] ms, and the resulting coincidence window dt_c (5.24 ms at bf = 0.20 -> 0.73 ms at bf = 1), supralinear gain (4.28x vs exact linear 2.000000), same-branch fraction (1.00 -> 0.00) and Pearson r(window, bf) = -0.698 (p = 0.054); transfer_boundary: 2D planar fan and 3D cubic Euclidean carrier-point greedy MST arbors with Rall 3/2 passive electrotonic diameter tapering mapped to Cajal's Golgi-stained Purkinje neuron drawing; the specimen reference values in `cajal_dendritic_wiring_reference.json` (D_f = 1.7817, Sholl peak = 73, hubs = 62) are the script's own Pass 2 outputs recorded as regression anchors, not independent literature morphometrics | (Pass 3) The dendritic Ca2+ hotspot is a single isopotential Morris-Lecar compartment with the canonical barnacle Hopf parameter set (C = 20 uF/cm2, Rinzel & Ermentrout 1998), not a Purkinje P-type Ca2+ channel fit; passive attenuation to the hotspot uses the semi-infinite-cylinder factor exp(-X), exact for Rall-matched equivalent cylinders viewed from the soma and approximate centripetally; the synaptic peak current I_peak = 110 uA/cm2 is a protocol choice tuned so single inputs stay sub-threshold | (Pass 3) Roth & Häusser (2001) passive constants and all five new DOIs were transcribed offline and are flagged as not re-verified in `electrotonic_ca_spike_reference.json`, the dataset README and the BibTeX file; does_not_claim: Purkinje P-type Ca2+ channel kinetics fitted to Llinás & Sugimori (1980) recordings, a multi-compartment active cable (NEURON-style) hotspot, Na+ dendritic spikes, activity-dependent filopodial synaptotropic retraction, or an empirical coincidence-window value from the literature (the 3 ms reference is tau_syn and the -1 reference is the predicted sign)]; other sources: Biophysics of Computation: Information Processing in Single Neurons <https://global.oup.com/academic/product/biophysics-of-computation-9780195181999>" ;
