    <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-hamant-microtubule-stress-meristem> a ns1:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Hamant et al. (2008), Bozorg et al. (2014), Sampathkumar et al. (2014), Uyttewaal et al. (2012), Heisler et al. (2010) & Louveaux et al. (2016) shoot apical meristem shell stress tensor, stress-vs-strain CMT alignment, katanin boundary morphogenesis and PIN1/CMT co-alignment on a growing L1 cellular vertex mesh faithfully reproduces Arabidopsis thaliana (shoot apical meristem L1 epidermal shell, organ-meristem saddle boundary, cylindrical stem, and epidermal cells) morphology" ;
        dct:description "The simulation script(s) 2008-hamant-microtubule-stress-meristem.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Arabidopsis thaliana (shoot apical meristem L1 epidermal shell, organ-meristem saddle boundary, cylindrical stem, and epidermal cells) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 71.0/100, audit 2026-10-19-microtubule-stress-meristem-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
        dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/d58a084/2008-hamant-microtubule-stress-meristem.py> ;
        ns1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/d58a084/2008-hamant-microtubule-stress-meristem.py; output: docs/media/microtubule_stress_meristem_comparison.png; organism: Arabidopsis thaliana (shoot apical meristem L1 epidermal shell, organ-meristem saddle boundary, cylindrical stem, and epidermal cells); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/d58a084; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: 347a8a2f33075229838f9d5d768d51cedd24b71fe8b15d8fd77294f559ac2ecb; artifact_sha256: f04ae6beca333c729a5589a05d79f4add33769b2697eeab9768fb92ce882708c; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/microtubule_stress_meristem/arabidopsis_meristem_boundary_specimen.jpg=3a8b54b71f7c780c..., datasets/microtubule_stress_meristem/microtubule_stress_meristem_reference.json=3e04a6dded528819..., datasets/microtubule_stress_meristem/paper_citations.bib=9009d0cffd73437a..., datasets/microtubule_stress_meristem/pin1_cmt_coalignment_reference.json=4d5ce4132c9e546d..., datasets/microtubule_stress_meristem/scout_manifest.json=e3cd62cc5263f317...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-microtubule-stress-meristem-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Hamant et al. (2008) Science 322:1650-1655 and Bozorg, Krupinski & Jönsson (2014) PLoS Comput. Biol. 10:e1003410: pressurized-shell / finite-element stress models of the SAM with isotropic stress on the central dome (K > 0), anisotropic tensile stress along the negative-Gaussian-curvature organ boundary (K < 0), 2:1 hoop-to-axial Laplace stress on the stem, circumferential CMTs around single-cell ablations, and the argument that maximal principal stress, not strain, is the directional cue | Uyttewaal et al. (2012) Cell 149:439-451 and Sampathkumar et al. (2014) eLife 3:e01967: katanin (BOT1/KTN1) sets the mechanical feedback gain; bot1/ktn1 and oryzalin reduce CMT ordering and flatten the boundary crease | Heisler et al. (2010) PLoS Biol. 8:e1000516: PIN1 polarity correlates with CMT orientation (PIN1-rich membranes parallel to the CMT array), both reorient after ablation (CMTs circumferential, PIN1 directed away from the wound) and the PIN1 response persists under oryzalin; hypothesis that PIN1 accumulates at the most stressed anticlinal wall | Louveaux et al. (2016) PNAS 113:E4294-E4303: SAM cells, especially at the organ boundary, orient division planes along maximal tensile stress, deviating from the Errera shortest-wall rule when stress is anisotropic; reconstructed_in_script: Section 2: 4-zone pressurized-shell stress table (prescribed sigma_1, sigma_2 per zone), nematic CMT order S = gamma a / (gamma a + 2 D_theta), stiffness anisotropy and viscoplastic Lockhart strain rates; exact integer checks of Lean Laws 1-3; an analytic (Gaussian-weight) 2D cross-section anisotropy field labelled 'FEM' in v2 | Section 3: 24-cell ring with prescribed angular dispersions for stress / strain / bot1 cues; closed-form 5-condition sweep of S, crease depth and ablation cos^2 fitted to the reference JSON (Pearson r = 0.9992 against the same JSON) | Section 3b (Pass 3): 2D L1 cellular vertex mesh — zone stress tensor as affine boundary pre-strain epsilon_i = sigma_i / E_shell; Hookean wall tensions; virial wall-stress tensor and wall-strain tensor per cell; CMT director relaxation with gamma_MT and S; cellulose reinforcement of CMT-parallel walls; softmax PIN1 allocation on wall tension; PIN1-polar auxin transport with diffusion, production and decay; auxin-enhanced Lockhart creep; division along sigma_1 (else Errera); T1 detection and flip (disabled by default); 9 scenarios with emergent PIN1/CMT co-alignment 0.747 (saddle WT), wound-ring CMT circumferential cos^2 0.985, PIN1 away-from-wound fraction 0.83 in WT and 0.83 under oryzalin, katanin sweep r = 0.995, 57 divisions with Euler residual 0 | Section 4: optical-density segmentation and Sobel/Gaussian structure-tensor coherency on the SHA-256-locked longitudinal SAM section (dome / saddle / stem ROIs); transfer_boundary: The vertex mesh is a tangent-plane (2D) approximation of the L1 sheet: curvature enters only through the boundary pre-strain, so dome vs saddle differ by boundary anisotropy, not by Gaussian curvature of a 3D surface; absolute S values on the mesh (dome 0.42, saddle 0.57, stem 0.59) are compared with but do not match the Hamant 2008 zone references (0.08, 0.81, 0.57) | The Heisler (2010) and Louveaux (2016) reference values used for the new measurements are within-30-degree ordinal thresholds (cos^2 >= 0.75) derived from the papers' qualitative statements, with 0.5 as the random null; no figure of either paper was digitised | The specimen is a stained longitudinal histological section, not an apical PIN1-GFP / MBD-GFP image, so its structure-tensor coherency measures cell-wall edge anisotropy in the section plane, not CMT or PIN1 organisation; does_not_claim: A 3D curved-surface vertex or FEM shell solver, the Bozorg (2014) stress-vs-strain discrimination as an emergent property of the mesh (both cues give circumferential CMTs around the wound here), auxin-maximum (phyllotactic) patterning from PIN1 up-the-gradient transport, subcellular katanin dynamics, or internal L2/L3 corpus mechanics]" ;
