        ns1:weightOfEvidence 8.69e-01 .

    <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux> a ns1:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Mitchison (1980, 1981) & Feugier, Mochizuki & Iwasa (2005) with-the-flux auxin canalization: exponent bifurcation, growing hex-lamina canalization (solved), Sachs wound bypass (solved + prescribed), hand-drawn Feugier venation templates, and cleared-leaf specimen segmentation faithfully reproduces Pisum sativum & Arabidopsis thaliana (dicot leaf lamina & stem vascular strands) morphology" ;
        dct:description "The simulation script(s) 1997-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Pisum sativum & Arabidopsis thaliana (dicot leaf lamina & stem vascular strands) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 70.1/100, audit 2026-10-19-auxin-canalization-leaf-veins-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
        dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/d58a084/1997-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux.py> ;
        ns1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/d58a084/1997-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux.py; output: docs/media/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins_comparison.png; organism: Pisum sativum & Arabidopsis thaliana (dicot leaf lamina & stem vascular strands); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/d58a084; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: c7349d3e4102c9f6306702949c507eeadd9b480c0de0ed099e56fb40eb8cea36; artifact_sha256: 7e4e81296d44001edb5c233e120b8436c10f86340b35a12fb0d14eb609089381; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins_reference.json=55e0f743ada83918..., datasets/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins/growing_lamina_reference.json=a51f3d788874ecb6..., datasets/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins/leaf_venation_canalization_specimen.jpg=7f67774708f8e952..., datasets/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins/paper_citations.bib=95f1b35cf42a8ae7..., datasets/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins/paper_citations_pass3.bib=b3a22b5ca5439009..., datasets/auxin_canalization_leaf_veins/scout_manifest.json=94a1d6a85fe649c5...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-auxin-canalization-leaf-veins-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Mitchison (1980) Proc. R. Soc. B 207:79-109: facilitated-diffusion canalization dD_ij/dt = alpha |phi_ij|^p + beta - gamma D_ij with phi_ij = D_ij (S_i - S_j); linear feedback gives diffuse flow, quadratic feedback gives winner-take-all 1-cell strands whose auxin concentration drops below the parenchyma (S_vein < S_par) | Mitchison (1981) Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B 295:461-471 & Sachs (1981) Adv. Bot. Res. 9:151-262: polar auxin transport canalization and C-shaped vascular bypass regeneration around a transverse stem incision | Feugier, Mochizuki & Iwasa (2005) J. Theor. Biol. 236:366-375: PIN1 carrier-allocation dynamics, open trees vs closed areoles; Feugier & Iwasa (2006) 243:235-244: canalization alone does not close loops | Scarpella et al. (2006) Genes Dev. 20:1015-1027 & Kang & Dengler (2004) Int. J. Plant Sci. 165:231-242: higher-order veins form progressively inside areoles bounded by lower-order veins; reconstructed_in_script: SOLVED (Pass 3): Mitchison (1980) facilitated-diffusion canalization with soft-saturating feedback alpha |phi|^2/(1+phi^2/phi_s^2) on a hexagonal cell lattice that divides 12x11 -> 24x22 -> 48x44 (75 -> 312 -> 1281 cells) with conductance inheritance; vein cells 7 -> 29 -> 155, nesting of new strands inside pre-existing panels 0.81 / 0.77, S_vein/S_par = 0.67 (emergent), sink intake / production 0.98, beta_1 = 0 at every stage (also 0 with the irreversible-differentiation ablation) | SOLVED (Pass 3): Sachs (1981) transverse incision on a 20x16 hex stem with a pre-established strand: sink intake 0.991 (incised, canalizing) vs 0.609 (alpha = 0 NPA control) vs 0.988 (intact), 6-cell lateral deflection; hop-count path ratio L_wounded/L_intact = 1.00 (metric insensitive to lateral deflection) | PRESCRIBED (Pass 2, retained and now labelled): 2-pathway exponent sweep p in {0.5, 1.0, 1.4, 2.0, 2.5} (the p = 1.4 point uses a special-cased eff_beta = 0.1766), analytic 12-column and 18x14 D/S profiles with hard-coded dip ratios (0.3105 / 0.6895), a Gaussian Sachs bypass centreline with constants 0.9852 / 0.0804, and two hand-drawn Feugier venation graphs (V=16/E=15 -> beta_1 = 0; V=28/E=41 -> beta_1 = 14) | Automated segmentation of the SHA-256-pinned cleared dicot leaf (primary midrib, secondary angle 52.93 +- 2.53 deg, 1843 closed areoles, vein areal coverage 0.416); transfer_boundary: Hexagonal cellular lattice with isotropic 2x refinement stands in for a growing leaf blade; strand orientations are quantised to ~30/60/90 deg so the branch-angle comparison with the specimen (52.9 deg) is lattice-limited | Facilitated diffusion (Mitchison 1980) is solved; polar PIN1 carrier allocation (Mitchison 1981 / Feugier 2005) is only verified algebraically (Law 3) and the hand-drawn graphs are not its output | Reference values 1.38 / 0.985 / 0.08 (Sachs) and the exponent-regime table in the pinned reference JSON are not digitised from any figure; they are carried as qualitative benchmarks; does_not_claim: Loop closure / areole formation or quaternary areole subdivision (beta_1 = 0 in every solved configuration; the specimen has 1843 areoles) | A finite-element or measured growth field; 3D mechanical blade expansion; polar PIN1 dynamics on a growing domain]" ;
        ns1:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2296(08)60351-1>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2005.03.017>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2006.05.022>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1086/382794>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.1980.0082>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.1981.0081>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1101/gad.1402406>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-313X.2005.02581.x>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.12253>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1145/1073204.1073251> ;
        ns1:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-code-traceability>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-model-fidelity>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-morphometric-validation>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-overall>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-parameter-expressivity>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-repro-package>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-source-traceability>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-mitchison-canalization-auxin-flux-specimen-match> ;
        ns1:weightOfEvidence 7.01e-01 .

    <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling> a ns1:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Moulton, Goriely & Chirat (2015) & Chirat et al. (2013) hierarchical mollusc aperture spine folding and fractal frond serration faithfully reproduces Murex pecten (Venus comb murex) & Chicoreus ramosus (ramose murex / Muricidae) morphology" ;
        dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2015-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Murex pecten (Venus comb murex) & Chicoreus ramosus (ramose murex / Muricidae) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 87.5/100, audit 2026-10-19-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
        dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/9987db7d/models/2015-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling.py> ;
        ns1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/9987db7d/models/2015-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling.py; output: docs/media/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling_comparison.png; organism: Murex pecten (Venus comb murex) & Chicoreus ramosus (ramose murex / Muricidae); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/9987db7d; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: 7e0a58a2882e57518d985e4253c9b131677e4c5f4943a638d01e321b11b48932; artifact_sha256: 1982f3f9822d1bbcf1b1f7746300e631c3f6c1877a192d1178f7c2019f4ea327; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling_reference.json=c73e5d2ac5c13cd5..., datasets/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling/murex_pecten_aperture_specimen.jpg=2f87d4ec30ebec6a..., datasets/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling/paper_citations.bib=fc39398ba54f69e1..., datasets/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling/pass3_koiter_cord_thickness_reference.json=63fdbb231492c336..., datasets/mollusc_aperture_spine_buckling/scout_manifest.json=53cb565add07b28d...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Chirat, Moulton & Goriely (2013): the mantle edge is an elastic strip growing against the calcified shell margin; excess growth gamma > gamma_c buckles it with wavelength lambda_1 ~ (E h_1^3 / 3 K_s)^(1/4) set by the mantle thickness and the foundation stiffness, giving comb spines along the siphonal canal of Murex pecten and varix spines in muricids | Moulton, Goriely & Chirat (2015): further growth folds each spine into a Kirchhoff elastica loop that self-contacts into a hollow tube, and thinner secondary/tertiary mantle generations produce the fractal frond cascade k_{i+1}/k_i = (h_i/h_{i+1})^(3/4) of Chicoreus ramosus | Rudraraju et al. (2019): the full problem is a finite-strain morphoelastic shell with surface and volume growth, solved by finite elements | Koiter (1966) and Manor, Hagberg & Meron (2008): the thin-shell energy used for the reduced surface model, and wave-number locking of a pattern-forming system to a spatially periodic forcing (Arnold tongues); reconstructed_in_script: Hierarchical wavenumber law k_i^* = (3 K_s / (E h_i^3))^(1/4); 1-D unilateral fourth-order comb energy minimised by L-BFGS-B at the rounded mode q_mode = 2 pi round(L/lambda_1)/L; parametric omega-loop spine profile with a linear neck-gap law; prescribed 3-stage frond cosines; prescribed 120 deg varix bursts; 10-species cross-taxa table; DoG specimen segmentation (Pass 2) | Pass 3: reduced linearised Koiter/FvK surface w(s,t) on a 192x96 bi-periodic torus (L_s = 67.2 mm, L_t = 24 mm, D = 1, K_0 = (k_1^*)^4) with varix growth window P(t), additive cord ridge forcing, semi-implicit gradient flow + L-BFGS-B relaxation from noise, emergent spine count / cord-intersection registration / ridge tilt; locking-tongue and obliquity sweeps; inverse-WKB h(u) from the specimen spacing profile; graded-rigidity 1-D comb with uniform control; allometric instar series with Brent root beta*; transfer_boundary: The Koiter surface is linearised (no finite membrane strain, flat reference torus, nondimensional D = 1 with outputs labelled mm) and the planar elastica cross-section is not coupled to it; both are separate modules, so the Pass 2 item 1 (3D finite-strain Koiter FE coupling) is addressed only in reduced form | h(s) is inferred from a Murex pecten photograph via inverse WKB (R^2 = 0.29, floor 0.35), not measured by micro-CT, and the species is not the Chicoreus ramosus the Pass 2 item asked for; no Chicoreus specimen is vendored | The 1-D comb spine count 16, the 120 deg varix spacing, the Chicoreus k_2/k_1 = 3.80, the neck-gap delta -3.1059 mm, the specimen CV 0.2092 reference, beta* = 4/3 and the rigid-spine 22.8 deg are prescribed or self-referential and must not be read as validations; does_not_claim: A finite-strain shell FE solution, a numerically integrated Kirchhoff-Clebsch elastica, emergent fractal frond serrations, emergent varix periodicity, measured mantle thickness data for any species, or sourced empirical k_2/k_1 and spine counts for the 10 muricid species]; other sources: On the nonlinear theory of thin elastic shells <https://dwc.knaw.nl/toegangen/digital-library-knaw/?pagetype=publist&search_author=PE00002224>" ;
        ns1:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2012.07.009>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2014.09.021>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1220443110>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1209/0295-5075/83/10005>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1007213> ;
        ns1:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-code-traceability>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-model-fidelity>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-morphometric-validation>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-overall>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-parameter-expressivity>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-repro-package>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-source-traceability>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-moulton-mollusc-aperture-spine-buckling-specimen-match> ;
        ns1:weightOfEvidence 8.75e-01 .

    <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-murray-mammalian-coat-markings> a ns1:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Murray (1981) Thomas substrate-inhibition reaction-diffusion pre-pattern, conical tail spot-to-ring transition & P1 surface-FEM felid body faithfully reproduces African leopard (Panthera pardus) coat rosettes and tapered tail markings morphology" ;
        dct:description "The simulation script(s) 1981-murray-mammalian-coat-markings.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real African leopard (Panthera pardus) coat rosettes and tapered tail markings specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 84.2/100, audit 2026-10-19-mammalian-coat-markings-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
        dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/64526a0/1981-murray-mammalian-coat-markings.py> ;
        ns1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/64526a0/1981-murray-mammalian-coat-markings.py; output: docs/media/mammalian_coat_markings_comparison.png; organism: African leopard (Panthera pardus) coat rosettes and tapered tail markings; checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/64526a0; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.4.2, scipy 1.17.0, matplotlib 3.10.8, Pillow 12.1.1; spec_fingerprint: b990576b2a4b3ccd36c08d1a21625f5ebd87766fc673675eaee0d8216821ffd3; artifact_sha256: 417dee8b24b004962c52143eefd329402da37f468ef4c9cbd844a2d13282558a; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/mammalian_coat_markings/felid_body_profile.json=65978462afc1b9e7..., datasets/mammalian_coat_markings/mammalian_coat_markings_reference.json=24d56069831e0ee9..., datasets/mammalian_coat_markings/panthera_pardus_specimen.jpg=86533a5dcf9cfee6..., datasets/mammalian_coat_markings/scout_manifest.json=28eabc9cf247b03e...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-mammalian-coat-markings-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Murray (1981): Thomas substrate-inhibition kinetics at a = 92, b = 64, alpha = 1.5, rho = 18.5, K = 0.1 are diffusively unstable for d > d_c; the domain scale factor gamma selects between no pattern, stripes, spots and crowding; on a tapered cylinder spot modes (m >= 1) are excluded below a critical radius so a spotted animal can have a ringed tail but not vice versa | Liu, Liaw & Maini (2006): leopard rosettes arise from a two-stage process in which a juvenile spot pre-pattern is re-run under changed parameters on the grown adult skin | Dziuk (1988), Pinkall & Polthier (1993), Dziuk & Elliott (2013): P1 surface finite elements with cotangent weights converge to the Laplace-Beltrami operator on a triangulated surface; reconstructed_in_script: Exact steady state, Jacobian, d_c, dispersion curve and Turing band; Neumann cylinder eigenmode counting including n = 0; r_crit (fastest-mode) and r_excl = 1/k_+ (full-exclusion) radii; fully implicit conical-tail PDE; planar DCT-II IMEX PDE run to saturation with a genuine two-stage protocol and growth-factor sweep; P1 surface FEM (stiffness/mass assembly, spectrum verification, two-stage run on the felid body) with annularity and spot-spacing diagnostics; DoG segmentation of the Kruger specimen; transfer_boundary: The 3D body is a parametric surface of revolution with hand-set knots (felid_body_profile.json), not a photogrammetric scan; its head-body length of 22 cub wavelengths and the growth factor G = 2 are ASSUMED, not fitted, which is why the FEM marking density (5.1 per torso depth) is 3.7x below the specimen estimate (18.9) | The tail ring onset comparison (0.82 measured vs 0.86 predicted) is on the surrogate taper, whose tip radius (0.004 HBL) was chosen to lie below r_excl; it is a consistency check of solver against linear theory, not a measurement on a real tail | The specimen is a 3/4-view photograph: the flank ROI is foreshortened and the v2 tail ROI covers hindquarters/background, so the Delaunay CV and polygonality are flank-only statistics; does_not_claim: That Murray (1981) or Liu et al. (2006) reported any of the FEM, annularity, growth-sweep or marking-density numbers; all are v3 reconstructions | That the Thomas kinetics are the biological mechanism of felid pigmentation]" ;
